\section{Module 3: Dual-Homed Compute \& Asymmetric Routing Management}

\subsection{Objective \& Architectural Challenge}
Module 3 deploys a multi-homed compute instance spanning both the DMZ and Private networks to act as an application gateway. A fundamental engineering challenge in multi-homed systems is \textbf{asymmetric routing} (Figure~\ref{fig:m3_asymm_concept}), wherein ingress packets arrive via one interface (DMZ with Floating IP) but response egress packets attempt to exit via a secondary interface (Private gateway), leading to packet drops by stateful firewalls.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\linewidth]{img/routing_traffic_flow.png}
\caption{Ingress and egress traffic flow for the dual-homed web instance under symmetric routing control.}
\label{fig:m3_asymm_concept}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Implementation Steps}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Security Group Provisioning (\texttt{module3-web-sg}):}
    Created a custom security group with ingress rules allowing TCP port 22 (SSH), TCP port 80 (HTTP), and ICMP (Ping) from any remote IPv4 prefix (\nolinkurl{0.0.0.0/0}), as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:m3_secgroup_rules}.
    
    \item \textbf{Dual-NIC Instance Provisioning (\texttt{module3-web-sever}) (Figure~\ref{fig:m3_instance_overview}):}
    Launched the instance with two dedicated virtual network interfaces:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textbf{NIC 1 (\texttt{eth1}):} Bound to \texttt{m2-dmz-net} with fixed IP \texttt{10.10.10.22}.
        \item \textbf{NIC 2 (\texttt{eth0}):} Bound to \texttt{m2-private-net} with fixed IP \texttt{10.10.20.137}.
    \end{itemize}
    Verification of active tenant network inventory prior to attachment is documented in Figure~\ref{fig:m3_networks_verify}.

    \item \textbf{Floating IP Association:}
    Allocated external public IP \texttt{172.24.4.187} from pool \texttt{public} and associated it \textbf{strictly} with the DMZ port (\texttt{10.10.10.22}), as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:m3_floating_ip}.
\end{enumerate}

