\subsection{Kernel Routing Configuration \& Service Verification}
Inside the guest operating system, network configuration and routing tables were inspected (Figure~\ref{fig:m3_ip_route}):
\begin{itemize}
    \item Interface \texttt{eth0} obtained \nolinkurl{10.10.20.137/24} while \texttt{eth1} obtained \nolinkurl{10.10.10.22/24}.
    \item To eliminate asymmetric routing drops, the kernel routing table was configured with a single primary default gateway:
\begin{lstlisting}[language=bash]
$ sudo ip route replace default via 10.10.10.1 dev eth1 metric 100
\end{lstlisting}
    This ensures all non-local outbound traffic, including HTTP responses to external clients, is returned through the DMZ gateway where DNAT state tables reside.
\end{itemize}

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.88\linewidth]{img/m3-instance-ip-route-terminal.png}
\caption{In-guest network configuration (\texttt{ip addr}) and kernel routing table (\texttt{ip route}).}
\label{fig:m3_ip_route}
\end{figure}

\noindent \textbf{End-to-End Service Delivery:}
A lightweight HTTP server was initialized via \texttt{sudo busybox httpd -f -p 80 -v}. External client requests were initiated against \url{http://172.24.4.187}. Figure~\ref{fig:m3_busybox_logs} demonstrates active ingress connections being served, and Figure~\ref{fig:m3_browser_http} displays the browser successfully rendering the verification page.

